{
 "id": "action-items",
 "title": "Extract Action Items",
 "pattern": "strings",
 "level": "medium",
 "statement": "<p>A meeting workspace lets people type notes during a call. Lines that start with <code>ACTION</code> or <code>TODO</code> should become tracked action items with owners and due dates. Everything else is ordinary notes.</p><p>Write <code>def solution(notes)</code>. <code>notes</code> is one string; lines are separated by <code>\\n</code>. Return a list with one dict per action item, in line order: <code>{\"owners\": [...], \"task\": \"...\", \"due\": \"YYYY-MM-DD\" or None}</code>.</p><p>A line is an action item when all of these hold:</p><ol><li>Strip the line. If it then starts with <code>\"- \"</code> or <code>\"* \"</code> (a bullet), remove those two characters.</li><li>It contains a <code>:</code>. The <i>head</i> is the text before the first <code>:</code>, the <i>body</i> is the text after it.</li><li>The first word of the head is <code>action</code> or <code>todo</code>, in any letter case.</li><li>The head has at least one more word, and every other word is an owner: <code>@</code> followed by at least one character, with no second <code>@</code>. Owner names are returned without the <code>@</code>, lower-cased, without duplicates, in first-mention order.</li><li>The task is the body's words joined by single spaces. If the body's last two words are <code>by</code> (any case) and a valid date, they are removed from the task and the date becomes <code>due</code>. A valid date is exactly <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code> in digits with month 01-12 and day 01-31. Otherwise <code>due</code> is <code>None</code> and the words stay in the task.</li><li>The task is not empty.</li></ol><p>Skip every line that fails a rule. Ordinary notes and malformed action lines are both skipped.</p>",
 "examples": [
  {
   "input": "notes = \"Agenda: Q4 launch\\n- ACTION @alice: send the deck by 2026-12-01\\nBob said the pilot went well.\\ntodo @Bob @carol: book room\\nTODO: tidy up\"",
   "output": "[{\"owners\": [\"alice\"], \"task\": \"send the deck\", \"due\": \"2026-12-01\"}, {\"owners\": [\"bob\", \"carol\"], \"task\": \"book room\", \"due\": None}]",
   "why": "Two action items. The bullet is dropped and owners are lower-cased. \"TODO: tidy up\" has no owner, so it is skipped; the other lines are plain notes."
  },
  {
   "input": "notes = \"ACTION @dan: call vendor by tomorrow\\nAction @eve: fix login by 2026-13-01\\nACTION @fay: by 2026-01-05\"",
   "output": "[{\"owners\": [\"dan\"], \"task\": \"call vendor by tomorrow\", \"due\": None}, {\"owners\": [\"eve\"], \"task\": \"fix login by 2026-13-01\", \"due\": None}]",
   "why": "\"tomorrow\" is not a date, so it stays in the task. Month 13 is invalid, so \"by 2026-13-01\" stays too. The last line's body is only a due date, which leaves an empty task: skipped."
  },
  {
   "input": "notes = \"action @Alice @alice @ALICE: draft memo at 10:30 by 2027-01-15\\nnotes: action @gus: not a prefix\\nACTION @ : bad owner\\nACTION alice: missing at sign\"",
   "output": "[{\"owners\": [\"alice\"], \"task\": \"draft memo at 10:30\", \"due\": \"2027-01-15\"}]",
   "why": "Three spellings of Alice collapse to one owner, and only the first colon splits, so \"10:30\" stays in the task. The other three lines fail: wrong first word, a bare @, and an owner without @."
  }
 ],
 "constraints": [
  "0 &le; len(notes) &le; 200,000 characters",
  "0 &le; number of lines &le; 10,000",
  "dates are not checked against month lengths: <code>2026-02-31</code> counts as valid"
 ],
 "starter": "def solution(notes):\n    # write your code here\n    pass\n",
 "think": [
  {
   "step": "Understand",
   "q": "What are the three parts of an action line?",
   "a": "A head (prefix plus <code>@owners</code>), a colon, and a body (the task, maybe ending in <code>by YYYY-MM-DD</code>). Each part has its own rule, so each can fail on its own."
  },
  {
   "step": "Small example by hand",
   "q": "<code>\"todo @Bob @carol: book room by 2026-12-01\"</code>?",
   "a": "Head <code>todo @Bob @carol</code>: prefix ok, owners <code>[\"bob\", \"carol\"]</code>. Body words end in <code>by</code>, <code>2026-12-01</code>: a valid date. Result <code>{\"owners\": [\"bob\", \"carol\"], \"task\": \"book room\", \"due\": \"2026-12-01\"}</code>."
  },
  {
   "step": "Naive idea",
   "q": "What would you reach for first?",
   "a": "One big regular expression. It can work, but it is hard to read in a live round, hard to change when the interviewer adds a rule, and optional groups behave differently across regex engines. Splitting into small string steps is easier to test."
  },
  {
   "step": "Which pattern?",
   "q": "Clue words?",
   "a": "\"parse\", \"prefix\", \"optional at the end\" &rarr; <b>string parsing with guard clauses</b>. Use <code>str.partition(\":\")</code>, <code>split()</code> and <code>lower()</code>, and return early as soon as a rule fails."
  },
  {
   "step": "Plan in plain steps",
   "q": "Write the steps.",
   "a": "<ol><li>Write <code>parse_line(line)</code> that returns a dict or <code>None</code>.</li><li>Strip, drop a bullet, <code>partition(\":\")</code>.</li><li>Check the prefix word, then every owner word.</li><li>Split the body; peel off <code>by DATE</code> if the last two words match.</li><li>Reject an empty task. In <code>solution</code>, keep the non-<code>None</code> results.</li></ol>"
  },
  {
   "step": "Edge cases to test",
   "q": "What could break?",
   "a": "No colon. <code>ACTION:</code> with no owner. A bare <code>@</code>. A colon inside the task (<code>at 10:30</code>: only the first colon splits). <code>by</code> followed by a bad date (stays in the task). A task that is only <code>by 2026-01-01</code> (empty). <code>@Alice</code> and <code>@alice</code> on the same line. Windows line endings (<code>strip()</code> removes <code>\\r</code>)."
  }
 ],
 "hints": [
  "Write a helper that parses one line and returns <code>None</code> for \"not an action item\". It keeps <code>solution</code> to three lines and makes each rule a single <code>if</code>.",
  "<code>head, colon, body = line.partition(\":\")</code> splits on the first colon only, and <code>colon</code> is <code>\"\"</code> when there is none.",
  "For the due date, look only at the last two body words: <code>words[-2].lower() == \"by\"</code> and an <code>is_date(words[-1])</code> helper that checks length 10, dashes at index 4 and 7, digits elsewhere, and the month and day ranges.",
  "Browser note: the in-browser Python here splits <code>' y'.split()</code> wrongly as <code>[' y']</code>. Call <code>.strip()</code> before <code>.split()</code>; real Python is fine either way."
 ],
 "solution": "PREFIXES = ('action', 'todo')\n\ndef is_date(word):\n    # YYYY-MM-DD with month 01-12 and day 01-31 (calendar lengths are not checked)\n    if len(word) != 10 or word[4] != '-' or word[7] != '-':\n        return False\n    y, m, d = word[:4], word[5:7], word[8:]\n    if not (y.isdigit() and m.isdigit() and d.isdigit()):\n        return False\n    return 1 <= int(m) <= 12 and 1 <= int(d) <= 31\n\ndef parse_line(line):\n    line = line.strip()\n    if line.startswith('- ') or line.startswith('* '):\n        line = line[2:]\n    head, colon, body = line.partition(':')\n    if not colon:\n        return None                          # no colon: not an action line\n    words = head.strip().split()\n    if not words or words[0].lower() not in PREFIXES:\n        return None\n    owners = []\n    for word in words[1:]:\n        name = word[1:].lower()\n        if not word.startswith('@') or not name or '@' in name:\n            return None                      # every word after the prefix must be @name\n        if name not in owners:\n            owners.append(name)              # dedupe, keep first mention order\n    if not owners:\n        return None\n    task_words = body.strip().split()   # strip first: the in-browser Python mis-splits ' y'\n    due = None\n    if len(task_words) >= 2 and task_words[-2].lower() == 'by' and is_date(task_words[-1]):\n        due = task_words[-1]\n        task_words = task_words[:-2]\n    if not task_words:\n        return None\n    return {'owners': owners, 'task': ' '.join(task_words), 'due': due}\n\ndef solution(notes):\n    items = []\n    for line in notes.split('\\n'):\n        item = parse_line(line)\n        if item is not None:\n            items.append(item)\n    return items\n",
 "brute": "def solution(notes):\n    # Brute force: a character-by-character scan of each line, no helper functions\n    result = []\n    for raw in notes.split('\\n'):\n        s = raw.strip()\n        if s[:2] in ('- ', '* '):\n            s = s[2:]\n        cut = -1\n        for i in range(len(s)):\n            if s[i] == ':':\n                cut = i\n                break\n        if cut < 0:\n            continue\n        parts = s[:cut].split()\n        if len(parts) < 2 or parts[0].lower() not in ('action', 'todo'):\n            continue\n        ok = True\n        people = []\n        for p in parts[1:]:\n            if len(p) < 2 or p[0] != '@' or p[1:].count('@') > 0:\n                ok = False\n            elif p[1:].lower() not in people:\n                people.append(p[1:].lower())\n        if not ok:\n            continue\n        rest = s[cut + 1:].split()\n        due = None\n        if len(rest) >= 2 and rest[-2].lower() == 'by':\n            d = rest[-1]\n            digits_ok = len(d) == 10 and d[4] + d[7] == '--' and (d[:4] + d[5:7] + d[8:]).isdigit()\n            if digits_ok and 0 < int(d[5:7]) < 13 and 0 < int(d[8:]) < 32:\n                due = d\n                rest = rest[:-2]\n        if rest:\n            result.append({'owners': people, 'task': ' '.join(rest), 'due': due})\n    return result\n",
 "explain": "<p>Split the job into \"parse one line\" and \"collect the results\". The line parser is a list of guard clauses, one per rule, so a new rule from the interviewer is one more <code>if</code>.</p><ul><li><code>- ACTION @alice: send the deck by 2026-12-01</code>: drop the bullet, partition on the colon, head words <code>[\"ACTION\", \"@alice\"]</code>.</li><li>Prefix <code>action</code> is allowed; owners <code>[\"alice\"]</code>.</li><li>Body words end with <code>by</code>, <code>2026-12-01</code>, a valid date: <code>due = \"2026-12-01\"</code>, task \"send the deck\".</li><li><code>TODO: tidy up</code> has a head of just <code>TODO</code>, so there is no owner and the line is skipped.</li></ul><p>In the round, say why you avoid a single regex: you can test <code>is_date</code> on its own, and each rejected line has an obvious reason.</p>",
 "complexity": "O(total characters) time and O(number of items) extra space",
